.he "chgrp (1)                    8/16/26                    chgrp (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
chgrp
- change group
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
chgrp [ -fR ] group file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Chgrp changes the group of each file to group, which may be a decimal
gid or a group name looked up in /etc/group.
.sp
Only the super-user may change a file's group.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-f	Do not report errors.

.ti -10
-R	Change a directory and everything under it, recursively.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The Berkeley chgrp let an owner who belonged to the new group change
their own file to it.  Micronix has no such membership test, and the
kernel lets only the super-user chown at all, so here chgrp is root's
tool; the check in the original was only advice.
.sp
A group name is looked up in /etc/group by hand, since libc has no
getgrnam.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1), chmod (1)
